      "review": {
        "summary": "The attempted transformation to use explicit shared memory allocation with tiling and vectorized loads/stores failed due to the unsupported Triton primitive 'shared_memory'. The prior version with simpler 2D tiling and tl.dot intrinsics improved performance significantly but still underutilizes the device compute capacity. Measured bottlenecks indicate compute underutilization and imperfect latency hiding; memory bandwidth is unlikely a bottleneck given low logical traffic. The failure to use shared memory explicitly suggests a limitation in the Triton environment or version used.",
        "measured_outcomes": "The baseline simt tiled kernel with (32,32,32) tiles, 8 warps, and 2 pipeline stages achieved ~2.8 TFLOPS on A100, a 2.7x speedup over naive. The attempted shared memory vectorized kernel with (64,64,64) tiles and 3 pipeline stages failed to compile due to unsupported primitive. No hardware counter bottlenecks detected; register usage is high but no spills. Latency hiding improved with pipelining but could be further optimized.",
        "failures": "The main failure is the usage of tl.shared_memory, which is not a supported primitive in the current Triton environment, causing a proposal error and no kernel launch.",
        "next_directions": [
          "Avoid using unsupported primitives like tl.shared_memory; use the officially supported shared memory patterns in Triton (e.g., via tl.shared.array).",
          "Implement shared memory tiling using supported Triton constructs such as tl.shared.array to stage tiles of A and B explicitly for data reuse.",
          "Increase tile sizes carefully within register and shared memory limits to improve arithmetic intensity and amortize memory latency.",
          "Increase pipeline stages to better hide latency if register pressure allows.",
          "Use vectorized load/store intrinsics supported by Triton to improve memory throughput and reduce instruction overhead.",
          "Explore warp-level cooperative loading and memory access coalescing to reduce divergence and improve memory efficiency.",
          "Profile and tune memory bandwidth usage explicitly to confirm bottlenecks and guide tile size adjustments.",
